{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.516,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.059
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Mixtures and their Separation"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 75"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.775,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": "The concentration of a solution can be expressed in several ways, a common one being the percentage which we are discussing here. You will learn about other methods in higher grades."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.139,
            0.774,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "The three main ways to express the concentration of a solution in terms of percentage are given below."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.182,
            0.588,
            0.2
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A. Mass by mass percentage (% m/m or % w/w)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.203,
            0.775,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "This method is commonly used to express the concentration of homogeneous mixtures. It tells us how many grams of solute are present in 100 grams of the total solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.257,
            0.512,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": "This can be expressed mathematically as,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.277,
            0.71,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "Mass by mass percentage  = \\frac{ Mass of solute }{ Mass of solution } \\times 100\\tag{5.1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.325,
            0.775,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "This method of expressing concentration is also used for heterogeneous mixtures, such as milk powder (Fig. 5.4a) and spice mixtures. It is also used to label the composition of packaged foods, showing how much salt, sugar, or protein is present in them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.399,
            0.774,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5.1:** If 10 g of salt is dissolved in 90 g of water, calculate the mass by mass percentage of the solution formed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.439,
            0.43,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:** Mass of salt (solute) = 10 g"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.46,
            0.412,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": "Mass of water (solvent) = 90 g"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.479,
            0.641,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "Total mass of solution = Mass of solute + Mass of solvent = 10 g + 90 g = 100 g"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.523,
            0.588,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": "Mass of solute Mass by mass percentage ×100 Mass of solution × % /g m m10 = 100 10 g100"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.606,
            0.598,
            0.626
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "B. Mass by volume percentage (% m/v or % w/v)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.628,
            0.775,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "This method is used where measuring the volume of a liquid is easier than weighing it, for example, in medicines and laboratories. A common example is 5% glucose solution (Fig. 5.4b). It tells us how many grams of the solute is present in 100 millilitres of the solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.699,
            0.29,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "Mathematically,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.718,
            0.628,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "Mass by volume percenta $\\mathrm{age} = \\frac{\\mathrm{Mass of solute}}{\\mathrm{Volume of solution}} \\times 100$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.71,
            0.729,
            0.752,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "(5.2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.762,
            0.776,
            0.798
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5.2:** If 5 g of glucose is dissolved in water to make 100 mL of solution, calculate its concentration in mass by volume percentage."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.8,
            0.454,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:** Mass of glucose (solute) = 5 g"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.82,
            0.399,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "Volume of solution = 100 mL"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.829,
            0.628,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &\\text { Number of solution - 100 nm} = \\frac{\\text { Mass of solute }}{\\text {Volume of solution }} \\times 100 \\\\&\\text { Mass by volume percentage } = \\frac{\\text { Mass of solute }}{\\text {Volume of solution }} \\times 100 \\\\&\\text { = } \\frac{5 \\text { g }}{\\text {100 nmL }} \\times 100 = 5 \\% \\text { nm/v }\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.798,
            0.093,
            0.834,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "**Next Level Up**"
        },
